import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> targetIndices(int[] nums, int target) {
        
        // Step 1: Sort the array in ascending order
        // This is required because we need indices AFTER sorting
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        // Step 2: Create a list to store the result indices
        // This will hold all positions where target appears
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

        // Step 3: Traverse the sorted array
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){
            
            // Step 4: Check if current element equals target
            if(nums[i] == target){
                
                // Step 5: If yes, add its index to the result list
                result.add(i);
            }
        }

        // Step 6: Return the list of indices
        return result;
    }
}
🧠 Simple Explanation
Sort the array
Scan it from left to right
Whenever you see target, store its index
Return all those indices
📌 Example
nums = [1,2,5,2,3], target = 2

After sorting:

[1,2,2,3,5]

Indices of 2:

[1, 2]

⏱️ Time Complexity
Sorting: O(n log n)
Loop: O(n)
➡️ Total: O(n log n) (perfectly fine for n ≤ 100)
